  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Surface high pressure remains in control \nlocally this afternoon through tonight. However, attention turns to \ncold-core closed upper-level low pressure currently making slow \nsouthward progress across the central Plains into the MO Valley. \nAttendant upper troughing sagging across the entirety of the \nSoutheast states with surface cyclogenesis underway across central \nFL. This area of low pressure expected to strengthen and make \nheadway into the Carolinas tonight, to the Ohio Valley by Thursday \nevening and push off to the northeast during the day Friday. This \nweekend into early next week, several potential northern and \nsouthern stream waves noted, none of which currently look to provide \nmuch in the way of notable impacts to northern MI.\n\nForecast Details: Little in the way of sensible weather anticipated \nthis afternoon through through the daytime hours Thursday. The most \nnotable change will be an increase in winds out of the east-northeast \non Thursday with the strongest gusts anticipated across northern \nlower -- ranging from 20-30 mph. Highs in the 40s area-wide for \nThanksgiving Day. Watching the aforementioned system make headway \ninto the Ohio Valley by late Thursday afternoon, and while the \ndeepest moisture will remain off to our southeast, chances for \nshowers enter the forecast across southeast areas by sunset. These \nchances spread farther north and west Thursday evening and overnight \nas somewhat better lift/moisture arrive locally. Expecting the most \nfrequent/numerous precipitation to favor areas east of I-75/US-127 \nin northern lower, especially the closer you get to Lake \nHuron/Saginaw Bay before this precip exits stage right Friday \nmorning through midday. Precip chances really trail off quickly as \nyou get towards the Straits and eastern U.P.\n\nThere remains a chance for rain to mix with or change over to snow \nThursday evening through Thursday night, primarily across the inland \nhigher terrain. Surface temperatures should be marginal for \naccumulation, and thus currently not anticipating much in the way of \nwidespread impacts. Suppose if a transition does occur and falls \nheavily enough for any length of time that a localized slushy accum. \ncould occur. Friday's highs back in the low-mid 40s across northern \nMI in the wake of this system's departure.\n\nThis weekend into early next week look to feature occasional waves \nthat may kick off light rain/snow showers at times, but nothing \nexpected to produce notable impacts at this time. Most likely timing \nfor those waves and attendant occasional precip chances look to be \nSaturday evening/night, late Sunday - Sunday night,  and again \ntowards the middle of next week.",
